%I A394658 #13 Oct 12 2026 00:18:55
%S A394658 56,135,375,686
%N A394658 Numbers k such that sigma(k) = psi(k) + tau(k) + Omega(k)^2.
%C A394658 The sequence is complete. Put F(k) := sigma(k) - psi(k) - tau(k) and L := Omega(k). For every nonsquarefree k with L >= 5, one has F(k) >= 2^(L-1) - L - 2, with equality at k = 2^L. This follows by induction from F(k) >= 9 for L = 5 and F(p*k) >= 2*F(k) + L + 1 for any prime p whenever k is nonsquarefree and F(k) >= 0. Since solutions require F(k) = L^2, the bound excludes L >= 7. Checking the remaining exponent partitions eliminates L = 5, 6 and shows that for L <= 4, solutions must have the form k = p^3*q, where p, q are distinct primes satisfying (p+1)*(q+1) = 24. This gives exactly the four listed terms. - _Max Alekseyev_, Oct 11 2026
%e A394658 56 is a term since sigma(56) = 120 = 96 + 8 + 4^2 = psi(56) + tau(56) + Omega(56)^2.
%o A394658 (PARI) isok(k) = {my(f = factor(k)); sigma(f) == prod(i=1, #f~, (f[i, 1]+1) * f[i, 1]^(f[i, 2]-1)) + numdiv(f) + bigomega(f)^2; } \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Mar 27 2026
%Y A394658 Cf. A000005, A000203, A001615, A001222.
